  "id": "Compound::DB01115 - Gene::781",
  "verbalization": "Nifedipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is consistently linked to Cacna2d1 through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Nifedipine, as part of the dihydropyridine class, binds to Cacna2d1, a gene involved in calcium channel regulation. This binding is supported by multiple paths, with the lowest common ancestor (LCA) consistently placing Nifedipine within the dihydropyridine family, which includes Isradipine and Nisoldipine, both of which also bind to Cacna2d1. The shared mechanism of action—modulating calcium channels—underpins the relationship between Nifedipine and Cacna2d1, as these molecules are involved in the same biological process. The evidence is robust, with multiple independent pathways converging on the same molecular interaction, reinforcing the mechanistic link. The presence of a well-documented mode of action, combined with the consistent binding of related compounds to Cacna2d1, supports the conclusion that Nifedipine exerts its effects through a direct interaction with Cacna2d1.",
